{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PARENTED TWO ORPHANED OPEN TICKETS INTO THIS EPIC. Direct children: 12 -> 14.\n\n  3768  story  open   Per-pass model configuration seam (one env var cannot express 4 passes)\n  5e75  task   open   Finish ADR 0056: extract build_agent_kwargs + the telemetry block\n\nBoth were this epic's OWN work products and were tracked only in prose (the handoff's \"WHAT IS\nLEFT\" list) plus a discovered_from link, so neither appeared as a child and neither counted\ntoward the epic's completion.\n\nWHY THESE TWO AND NOT THE OTHER 20. Of 1630 tickets, 697 have no parent; 124 of those are not\nclosed; 22 are open task/story. I content-searched all 22 descriptions (not just titles) for\nprovider-seam vocabulary -- provider seam, providers.py, bedrock, model class, ProviderSession,\nModelProfile, provider_provenance, model_ladder, resolve_model_string, per-pass model. Exactly two\nmatched:\n  3768 on bedrock / model class / model_ladder / per-pass model. It already carried\n       discovered_from->061c and blocks->eb6e (S8) and blocks->0d76 (S6), i.e. it already gated\n       two of this epic's own stories.\n  5e75 on ProviderSession, via discovered_from->7f86 -- and 7f86 IS this epic's ADR 0056 story\n       (Gerrit 1122, in this epic's stack).\nThe other 20 are unrelated subsystems: Jira reconciler internals, git call-site bounds, plan-review\ncriteria tuning, docs, and probes. Not parented, deliberately.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY: this epic's AC is \"every child story is closed\", so both now GATE the\nclose. For 3768 that changes nothing in practice -- its blocks->eb6e/0d76 links already made it a\nprerequisite. For 5e75 it is a REAL scope change: it was filed as a tracked follow-up, and it is\nnow mandatory before this epic can close. Reversible with `rebar edit 5e75 --parent=` if the\noperator intends it as non-gating.\n\nWHY NOW RATHER THAN LATER, which is the non-obvious part: a ticket's PARENT is part of its\nmaterial fingerprint (attest.py builds PlanContext with children and hashes it). Both tickets are\ncurrently UNSIGNED -- verified, not assumed -- so parenting destroys nothing. Doing it AFTER either\none earns a plan review would have made that fresh attestation stale-material, the same trap\nAGENTS.md records for set-file-impact. Parent before reviewing, never after.\n\nAdding children also changes THIS epic's own material fingerprint. That costs nothing here because\nthe epic's attestation was ALREADY stale-code before this edit: `review-plan 061c --status`\nreported 18 drifted dependency files (docs/env-vars.md, docs/event-schema.md, docs/llm-framework.md,\ninfra/terraform/iam_s7.tf, pyproject.toml, ...) against its signing sha 7fc96b277. A fresh epic\nplan review is required before close regardless of this change.\n\nSEPARATE FINDING, NOT ACTED ON (out of scope: open task/story only). Story 7f86 -- this epic's\nADR 0056 decomposition, Gerrit 1122, sitting in this epic's own review stack -- is CLOSED with\nparent=None, so its whole subtree (e6bd, 8229, a49e, all closed, all correctly parented to 7f86)\nhangs off the epic entirely. Four closed bugs this epic produced are likewise detached: a574 and\n1903 (both blocks->eb6e), 281f (discovered_from->2932) and 3a98 (discovered_from->061c). The\nhandoff's \"CLOSED (22)\" tally counts these as epic work, so the epic's real delivered scope is\nwider than its child list shows. One `rebar edit 7f86 --parent=061c...` would reattach the largest\npiece."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785554787698436001,"uuid":"816623bf-aa7f-4b56-926c-88ab447d7f73"}